Caller usage:

```rust
// Service boundary captures time once.
let as_of = Utc::now();
history.map(|history| summarize_member(history, as_of))

// Pure summary; tests supply an explicit timestamp.
summarize_member(history, utc("2026-10-23T12:00:00Z"))
```

```svelte
{
  label: `Declared amounts in ${dateLabelYear(summary.declaredTotals.calendarYearStart)}`,
  total: summary.declaredTotals.calendarYear,
}
```

Append this to the existing metric collection; retain `Metric` properties and change empty metric text to `"No declarations recorded"`.

Types and signatures:

```rust
/// UTC interval [January 1, January 1 of following year).
/// Private boundaries; construction establishes ordering and calendar alignment.
struct CalendarYearPeriod {
    start: DateTime<Utc>,
    end: DateTime<Utc>,
}

impl CalendarYearPeriod {
    fn containing(as_of: DateTime<Utc>) -> Self;
    fn includes(&self, registered_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>) -> bool;
}

fn summarize_member(
    history: MemberHistory,
    as_of: DateTime<Utc>,
) -> MemberDetails;
```

Extend `DeclaredGbpTotals` with:

```rust
pub calendar_year_start: DateTime<Utc>,
pub calendar_year: DeclaredGbpPeriodTotal,
```

Inside the existing declaration loop, observe each declaration into a third `PeriodAggregate` when its timestamp is in the calendar interval. Reuse `gbp_total()` unchanged.

Files:

- `domain/services/entity_details.rs`: calendar interval, timestamp parameter, aggregate.
- `domain/models/entity_details.rs`: mandatory calendar fields.
- `inbound/http/handlers/entity_details.rs`: serialize those fields with existing decimal handling.
- `frontend/src/lib/entity-details.ts`: corresponding required camelCase fields.
- `frontend/src/lib/server/entity-details.ts`: parse required fields using existing timestamp/period parsers.
- `frontend/src/MemberIdentity.svelte`: append metric and update empty text.
- Existing backend summary tests and affected frontend tests: calendar boundaries, undated exclusion, exact amounts, empty state.

Rationale: the interval directly owns registration membership and avoids duplicating year-number comparisons across layers. Its start timestamp supplies the displayed year, so a separate year field cannot drift from the period. UTC and half-open boundaries handle year transitions precisely. All registered records within that year qualify, including future-dated records before the next January 1; undated declarations remain historical only. GBP, completeness, category coverage, counts, and precision continue through the existing aggregator.
